If total market welfare were the sole objective, any price between the seller’s cost ($200) and Lucas’s willingness to pay ($506) would be welfare‑maximizing, as total surplus is buyer valuation − seller cost and does not depend on the trade price. In practice, I would have reported an offer of $506—since my client can pay up to that amount—and noted that choosing a lower price would simply shift surplus between buyer and seller without changing the total.

I truthfully reported Lucas’s limits and situation.  

